    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d570-a2">

                <pb facs="0796" n="776" sameAs="frus:frus1864p2/pg776"/>

                <head>By telegraph from collector at Port Colborne to honorable A. T. Galt.</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1864-11-08"><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >November</hi> 8, 1864.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>Propeller Georgiana entered inwards from Port Stanley on 29th of October,
                    discharged cargo and reported inwards for Buffalo on 1st of November; returned
                    here on 3d; captain came to the office and stated he had been to Buffalo for
                    repairs; had a clearance for this port without cargo; was going to Sarnia, but
                    wanted wood; wished to know if it was necessary to have a clearance from here to
                    Sarnia, having a clearance to a British port; did not grant another; weather
                    stormy; remained until Saturday, when weather moderated; left at six thirty<pb
                        facs="0797" n="777" xml:id="pg777"/>p. m.; on inquiring of harbor-master, he
                    informed me she took sixty or seventy cords wood, as stated, for ballast; he
                    stated they informed him on board she was going to Chicago; nothing connected
                    with her suspicious unless taking the quantity of wood and going to one port,
                    when reporting here she was going to another, might be so construed; did not
                    come to the office after taking wood on board; Mr. Gordon, from whom the wood
                    was purchased, has stated she only took thirty cords.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>N. PRING, <hi rendition="#italic">Collector.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
